# 0003. Startup is silent and fast: no process per shell, tool inits cached

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-11

## Context
`eval "$(tool init zsh)"` and generated completions start a process on every shell, and
compinit re-scans fpath every time. Any output before the prompt breaks p10k's instant prompt.

## Decision
- Budget ~50 ms to a usable prompt, measured with `make bench`; `make check` fails on any output.
- `_cached_source` writes a tool's init to `~/.cache/zsh/<tool>.zsh` (zcompiled) and regenerates it
  only when the tool's binary is newer; `_cached_completion` does the same for completions, in the
  background.
- `compinit` does a full check once a day, otherwise trusts its dump (`compinit -C`).
- Process-free replacements where a tool's init would spawn one (atuin's session id is a UUIDv7
  made in zsh).
- No network at startup.

## Alternatives
- Plain `eval "$(tool init zsh)"`: simplest, ~10 ms per tool per shell.
- evalcache-style plugins: another dependency for a 10-line function.

## Consequences
- Fast, quiet startup with p10k instant prompt.
- Stale caches: after changing how a tool is initialised, `make clean`; new completions show up
  the next day or after `make clean`; the first shell after installing a tool lacks its completion.
- The atuin UUID block must follow atuin's format if it changes.

## References
`conf.d/30-completion.zsh`, `conf.d/40-tools.zsh`, `Makefile`, CLAUDE.md gotchas 2, 3, 5.
